WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Brian&nbsp;
from Ireland. Brian, welcome.&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.180
Speaker 3: Are you, David, sir? How you doing.
Speaker 1: Sir? Welcome to the program.&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:15.600
Speaker 3: Yeah. First time caller. Thanks a lot.&nbsp;
I was on say thanks very much for. For covering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.360
these debates on the livestreams. It's really&nbsp;
interesting for someone looking from abroad at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.389
the American system. It's pretty interesting.
Speaker 1: Thank you.&nbsp;

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:28.800
Speaker 3: And my question relates to that as&nbsp;
well. Let's say if somebody in one of these TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.780
networks put you in charge of arranging the&nbsp;
debate, what kind of changes would you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.400
to the format? Because and I think you're in&nbsp;
agreement with me here that it's intensely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.960
frustrating. You know, there's never enough time&nbsp;
to develop candidates positions, never enough time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.140
to scrutinize. When Charlie Crist Charlie Crist,&nbsp;
try to ask the scientists some basic questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.640
You know, about, well, he served four years. Yes.&nbsp;
And completely ignored him. And that was allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.129
by the moderators. No pushback whatsoever.&nbsp;
So how would you arrange this kind of thing?&nbsp;

00:00:58.129 --> 00:01:03.780
Speaker 1: So first first and foremost, there have&nbsp;
to be exchanges between the candidates. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.820
last night's debate between DeSantis and Crist,&nbsp;
Crist kept trying to get the scientist to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.880
I will or I won't commit to serving all four&nbsp;
years. And DeSantis just looked like his brain was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.860
malfunctioning. And then the moderator would jump&nbsp;
in and say, We have agreed that the candidates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.600
will not ask each other questions or address each&nbsp;
other. They will only talk to the moderator. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.060
the candidates seemed to have insisted on that.&nbsp;
Sometimes it's the moderators and the organizers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:36.180
organizers who put that in. I think the way we're&nbsp;
really going to learn more is we need longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:42.060
periods of less structured back and forth between&nbsp;
the candidates. Bring up the issue of abortion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.980
for example. Frame it with a question, but&nbsp;
then it doesn't have to be a half hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.560
but at least allow 5 minutes of open back&nbsp;
and forth with the moderator being there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.720
to be able to jump in and say, well, hold&nbsp;
on a second, don't talk over each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:04.920
Crist just asked DeSantis this specific question.&nbsp;
DeSantis Can you answer that? And sort of managing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:11.160
the unstructured time to make sure everybody&nbsp;
gets their points across. We would learn so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.560
more from that than from 60 seconds. Now, you&nbsp;
respond to me for 30 seconds, but don't talk to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:23.280
each other. It's just it's too short. It's not the&nbsp;
way we really make progress by talking through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.600
moderator, talk to each other, but in a way where&nbsp;
we can actually hear what everybody's saying.&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.600
Speaker 3: Yeah, I completely agree.
Speaker 1: That's the fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.660
change. I would make more time on&nbsp;
each topic and talk to each other.&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.469
Speaker 3: I don't see how anyone halfway&nbsp;
sensible could disagree with you on that and just.&nbsp;

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:42.360
Speaker 1: One minute seem not to want it.&nbsp;
You know, I think they see it as very risky.&nbsp;

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.100
Speaker 3: The last thing for me as well.&nbsp;
I also wouldn't have a live audience there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.820
because it ends into a cast as well, but.
Speaker 1: It was totally farcical. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.800
absolutely. Brian from&nbsp;
Ireland, appreciate the call.&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.560
Speaker 3: Thanks, David.
Speaker 1: All right. Appreciate getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.780
to speak to some of you today. We'll go to a quick&nbsp;
break and be back with more show right after this.

